-**Parameters and Edge Cases:**
-*   **Sample Spacing:** Must be chosen to balance performance and quality [S6, book]. Too large causes aliasing; too small wastes bandwidth [S3, unofficial].
-*   **View Direction Parallel to Slices:** If the view direction is parallel to the primary axes of pre-computed slices, the projection distorts the slice views, degrading image quality [S4]. In the extreme case where the view direction is parallel to the slices, no image is generated [S4].
-*   **Aliasing:** Lack of interpolation between slices may lead to aliasing artifacts if the scalar field or transfer functions contain high frequencies [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Popping Artifacts:** Sudden changes in slicing direction can cause popping [S3, unofficial]. This is worse for anisotropic data [S3, unofficial]. Interpolating in-between slices mitigates this [S3, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Axis Switching:** When the view direction crosses the 45-degree threshold between axes, the active texture stack changes abruptly. This causes "popping" artifacts (sudden changes in pixel intensity) because the sampling density and direction change discontinuously [S4, S8, unofficial].
+*   **Aliasing:** Lack of interpolation between slices (only bilinear within slices) can cause aliasing if the volume or transfer function has high frequencies [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Edge-On View:** If the view direction is parallel to the primary axes of the pre-computed slices, the projection distorts the slice views, degrading image quality. If parallel to the slices, no image is generated [S9].
+*   **Precision:** The method is equivalent to standard ray casting only if precision is not lost due to integer arithmetic in the compositing step [S9].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Distorted Video Volumes (Horseshoe Mapping)](7004-distorted-video-volumes-horseshoe-mapping.md)
-This method renders deformed volumetric data, specifically video volumes bent into a horseshoe shape, by distorting texture coordinates rather than geometry. It enables the use of standard planar, view-aligned texture slicing (as described in [Image-Parallel Ray Casting via 2D Texture Mapping](5854-image-parallel-ray-casting-via-2d-texture-mapping.md)) on non-planar data by computing inverse mappings in the fragment shader. Unlike forward mapping, it avoids gaps and overlaps inherent in geometry deformation, ensuring every pixel is covered by a valid texture lookup.
+Renders deformed volumetric data by distorting texture coordinates to allow standard planar slicing on non-planar data.
