 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering speed depends only on image size, not scene complexity [S1, unofficial]. It is generally faster than software ray casting for large volumes [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing three copies of the volume in texture memory (one for each slicing direction) [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** 2D texture slicing outperforms 3D texture slicing for large volumes due to coherent memory accesses and higher performance of bi-linear interpolation compared to tri-linear [S3, unofficial]. Performance factors include software sampling rate, texture download rate, and polygon scan conversion rate [S6, book].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, leverages standard graphics hardware, fast rendering of large datasets, no complex geometric models required [S1, unofficial][S4].
-*   **Limitations:** Lower image quality compared to 3D texture mapping if only trilinear interpolation is used on 3D data [S5]. Distortion artifacts if pre-computed slices are used for arbitrary viewpoints [S4]. Popping artifacts when switching slicing directions [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Large volumetric datasets where interactive frame rates are required and 3D texture memory is insufficient or too slow [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When high image quality is critical and 3D texture mapping is available, or when the view direction is frequently parallel to the primary axes of pre-computed slices [S4].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of slices and $P$ is the number of pixels covered by the projected volume. The rendering speed depends only on image size, not scene complexity [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $3 \times W \times H \times D$ (in terms of texture storage), as three full copies of the volume are required [S2][S4, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On an nVidia GeForce 7800 GT, rendering a 35x35x35 lattice with slice spacing 0.2 and ~90,000 pixels achieved 197 FPS (no noise) to 30 FPS (complex noise) [S10, unofficial]. 2D texture slicing generally outperforms 3D texture slicing for large volumes due to better cache locality and hardware optimization for 2D textures [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Works on hardware with only 2D texture support (e.g., early GPUs, some mobile devices) [S1][S8, unofficial]. Allows real-time modification of colormaps via palette modification if using indexed colors [S5].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory usage (3 copies). Visual artifacts (popping, aliasing) when view direction changes or is edge-on. Less flexible than ray-casting methods [S1, S5].
+*   **When to Use:** When 3D texture hardware is unavailable, or for very large volumes where 3D texture memory limits are prohibitive and 2D paging is acceptable [S5][S8, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When high-quality, artifact-free rendering across all view angles is required, or when memory bandwidth is a bottleneck [S4, S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originated as a solution to the lack of 3D texture mapping hardware in early graphics pipelines [S2]. Historically, it was used when 3D texture mapping was unavailable and remains relevant today for large volumes due to its performance characteristics [S3, unofficial]. The approach was formalized by Cullip and Neumann (1993), Guan and Lipes (1994), and Cabral et al. (1994) [S3, unofficial]. Rezk-Salama et al. (2000) improved the method by addressing popping artifacts and opacity correction [S3, unofficial].
+The method was devised by Cabral et al. [1994] and ran on SGI Reality Engine workstations [S6, unofficial]. It was one of the first texture-based volume rendering techniques, leveraging the graphics hardware's texture mapping capabilities before 3D texture mapping became standard [S6, S8, unofficial]. It is historically significant as a bridge between software ray casting and modern GPU-based volume rendering, particularly for systems lacking 3D texture units [S2][S8, unofficial].
 
